WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.520
Shark attacks, mid-air collisions, and&nbsp;
plummeting to their death. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.640
these stunt actors lost their lives&nbsp;
making these movie scenes a reality.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.400
Harry O'Connor, who served as a stunt&nbsp;
double for Vin Diesel in "xXx," died on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.600
the Prague set of the film while performing a&nbsp;
paragliding stunt in his place. An experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.600
skydiver and stunt performer who'd joined&nbsp;
the industry after retiring as a Navy SEAL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.680
O'Connor reportedly collided with a pillar&nbsp;
of the Palacky Bridge while paragliding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.560
and broke his neck in the process before&nbsp;
passing away. It was his second effort to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.280
complete the stunt, the first of which&nbsp;
was successful and used in the movie.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.840
Director Rob Cohen later&nbsp;
said on his commentary track,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.520
"We had 500 stuntmen involved with this picture;&nbsp;
499 didn't get a scratch. It shows you the lengths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.280
to which we will go to bring this kind of&nbsp;
intense experience to the viewer. Stuntmen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.800
know they are in danger. They make their&nbsp;
living through danger. Most of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.840
it's all right. Sometimes,&nbsp;
unfortunately, it isn't."

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.240
O'Connor was 44.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.000
"You are America's best. Make us proud."

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.720
Stunt pilot Art Scholl had become internationally&nbsp;
renowned for his aerial stunt work in films like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.800
"Blue Thunder" and "The Right Stuff" before he was&nbsp;
selected to complete an inverted air stunt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.200
would become a big part of the storyline in "Top&nbsp;
Gun." According to The Los Angeles Times, Scholl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.440
was heard relaying his in-air troubles during&nbsp;
production on the scene, saying on his radio,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.480
"I've got a problem here."

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.200
Scholl was highly skilled at the maneuver,&nbsp;
so colleagues concluded that there must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.720
have been a mechanical failure that&nbsp;
caused his vessel to crash. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:28.040
neither he nor his plane were recovered&nbsp;
from the debris, despite a search effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.640
so the details leading up&nbsp;
to his death are unknown.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.000
The mystery that surrounds the&nbsp;
failure that cost him his life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.120
still lingers with his widow, Judy&nbsp;
Scholl, who told The Daily Mail,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.000
"Art didn't say what the problem was. This is&nbsp;
the one thing I'm not happy about with him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.960
So I don't know if he had a control failure,&nbsp;
where maybe the camera jammed into the flight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.320
controls. Or whether he had an issue with&nbsp;
the flight controls where he wasn't able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.520
to get the airplane out of the flat spin or&nbsp;
whether he was disorientated. He didn't say."

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.280
Scholl was 53.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.120
In one of the grisliest accidents to ever&nbsp;
occur on a Hollywood set, stuntman Jose Marco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.840
a double for Burt Reynolds, was brutally attacked&nbsp;
by a real-life shark while shooting an underwater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.320
scene for "Shark!" Marco was filming alongside&nbsp;
a subdued bull shark in scuba gear when a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.000
white managed to make it through the nets&nbsp;
protecting the area from the rest of the sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.920
and charged at the camera crew before launching at&nbsp;
Marco and disemboweling the actor where he swam.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.200
Crew members tried to steer the shark away from&nbsp;
Marco with spears, but the animal was undeterred,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.880
and the stuntman's resulting injuries were&nbsp;
so severe that he died shortly after being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.520
transported to the hospital. The incident&nbsp;
was catastrophic, but producers wanted to and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.960
ultimately did use the footage of Marco's death&nbsp;
in the movie, along with publicizing the incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.000
as a marketing push. Director Samuel Fuller&nbsp;
was so disgusted that he disavowed the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.040
unsuccessfully attempting to have his name&nbsp;
removed from its credits. Marco was 32.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.440
The Vancouver, Canada set of "Deadpool 2" became&nbsp;
the site of a tragedy in August 2017 when a stunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.480
woman named Joi "SJ" Harris was killed while&nbsp;
attempting to complete a motorcycle sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.520
for the film. Harris was substituting for&nbsp;
actress Zazie Beetz as the character Domino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.040
and was not wearing a helmet during the scene&nbsp;
in question to match the character. Harris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.560
had successfully completed the stunt four&nbsp;
times in a row, but on the fifth attempt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.760
she lost control of the vehicle and went&nbsp;
airborne before careening into a glass building.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.080
Local news crews reported that she was&nbsp;
treated on site by paramedics after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.560
incident, but was later taken to the&nbsp;
hospital and ultimately declared dead.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.760
In a statement, a rep for 20th&nbsp;
Century Fox said of the incident,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.040
"We are deeply saddened by the&nbsp;
accident that occurred on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.280
set of 'Deadpool 2' this morning. Our&nbsp;
hearts and prayers are with the family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.320
friends and colleagues of our crew&nbsp;
member during this difficult time."

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.120
Lead actor Ryan Reynolds echoed that sentiment,&nbsp;
writing on X, formerly known as Twitter,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.960
"We're heartbroken, shocked and devastated&nbsp;
... but recognize nothing can come close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:39.320
the grief and inexplicable pain her family&nbsp;
and loved ones must feel at this moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.480
My heart pours out to them — along with each&nbsp;
and every person she touched in this world."

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:47.520
Angela Bassett's double in "Vampire in Brooklyn"&nbsp;
had invited her mother and siblings to the set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.080
of the film to watch her work on the day her&nbsp;
life was cut short. Sonja Davis' mother Wanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.640
Sapp would later reveal that she overheard&nbsp;
her daughter casting doubt on the safety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:00.080
the stunt she was about to perform during their&nbsp;
fateful visit, telling The Los Angeles Times,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.760
"The last words I heard my baby say was when&nbsp;
she yelled down to the stunt coordinator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.000
'Are you sure?' I could feel Sonja&nbsp;
wasn't comfortable with the stunt."

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.720
Davis had been performing a 42-foot backward&nbsp;
jump off a building, but rather than landing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.680
safely on the airbag that was supposed&nbsp;
to cushion her fall, she instead bounced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.880
back into the building before making impact&nbsp;
with the ground, much to her family's horror.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.760
Her mother initiated a multi-million dollar&nbsp;
wrongful death suit against the studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.960
Paramount Pictures, along with director&nbsp;
Wes Craven and producer/star Eddie Murphy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.200
but the case was ultimately dismissed.&nbsp;
Stunt coordinators on the film would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.320
later claim in an oral history of the movie&nbsp;
that Davis didn't perform the jump correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:40.880
but there were also no ambulances on&nbsp;
set during the incident. Davis was 32.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:47.760
"One More! Rest in pieces."

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.800
The "Expendables" movies were envisioned as&nbsp;
a vehicle for pure action-driven spectacle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.440
bringing together the biggest stars in&nbsp;
the genre. Led by Sylvester Stallone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.040
the films have featured such heavyweight action&nbsp;
heroes as Arnold Schwarzenegger, Bruce Willis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:05.560
Chuck Norris, Jet Li, Wesley Snipes, and many&nbsp;
more. But with all that action comes the increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.320
risk of disaster, and tragically that's just&nbsp;
what happened on the set of "The Expendables 2."

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.880
During production of a second unit&nbsp;
water scene on Lake Ognyanova, Bulgaria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:18.400
stunt actor Kun Liu was killed and fellow&nbsp;
double Nuo Sun was severely injured when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.880
planned explosion went awry. The pair were riding&nbsp;
on an inflatable rubber raft at the time. Liu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.720
was reportedly doubling for Stallone himself,&nbsp;
while Sun was subbing for Jet Li in the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.560
unit shot. Liu was just 26 at the time of his&nbsp;
death and the film was dedicated to his memory.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.720
Sun managed to survive his serious&nbsp;
injuries and filed suit against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.960
Sylvester Stallone and Millennium&nbsp;
Films for damages. The family of Liu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:42.240
meanwhile, also pursued wrongful death damages&nbsp;
against the parties responsible for the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.080
including stunt coordinator Chad Stahelski,&nbsp;
who went on to direct the "John Wick" films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.520
In 2017 it was reported that the plaintiffs&nbsp;
were awarded $195,000 Hong Kong dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.360
while Jet Li himself gave $5.8 million&nbsp;
Hong Kong dollars to the stuntman's family.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:01.320
Written and directed by the late schlock auteur&nbsp;
Albert Pyun, the 1982 sword-and-sandal adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.240
"The Sword and the Sorcerer" starred Lee&nbsp;
Horsley as Prince Talon, who learns he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.160
descended from noble blood and must take on a&nbsp;
powerful and evil wizard. Though it's not one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:13.440
of that decade's most memorable movies in the&nbsp;
genre, it made the news after one of the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.840
performers — 37-year-old John Hale "Jack" Tyree&nbsp;
— died in a stunt gone wrong during production.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.560
Tyree was a veteran of the industry who'd&nbsp;
worked on everything from "Escape From New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.840
York" to "History of the World: Part I." He was&nbsp;
filming a scene in Hollywood's Griffith Park for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.200
"The Sword and the Sorcerer," in which he was&nbsp;
to jump from an 80-foot cliff. He unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.120
landed a few feet away from the airbag intended&nbsp;
to cushion his fall, causing severe injuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.000
that eventually cost him his life. As reported by&nbsp;
The Calgary Herald, an official said at the time,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.960
"It was definitely a high-risk stunt. But&nbsp;
Jack was a real professional. He had some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.960
more difficult high falls before, tougher than the&nbsp;
one he faced [on 'The Sword and the Sorcerer']."

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.880
Perhaps surprisingly, the stunt&nbsp;
itself was left in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:56.440
despite Tyree's tragic passing, and the&nbsp;
film was dedicated to him in the credits.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:00.440
The James Bond franchise has built a reputation&nbsp;
for its impressive stunts and elaborate action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:04.440
sequences. Each film in the series seems to&nbsp;
attempt to top the one before it, and to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.880
that can sometimes mean making the stunts riskier&nbsp;
and riskier. But by the time the franchise got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.600
its 12th entry in 1981, "For Your Eyes Only,"&nbsp;
the risk-taking may have gone a little too far.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:19.040
To create one skiing and bobsledding chase that&nbsp;
takes place amidst the wintery slopes of Italy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.320
the film involved the use of a four-man bobsled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.560
operated by the second unit director to better&nbsp;
capture footage of the action. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.640
23-year-old Italian stunt driver Paolo&nbsp;
Rigoni fell off the sleigh and was quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:33.520
killed after it overturned and dragged&nbsp;
him underneath. It wasn't the first or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:37.960
last serious injury to happen on the set of&nbsp;
a James Bond film, but it was the deadliest.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:41.960
It's been reported that it was his death — along&nbsp;
with the death of professional bobsledder James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.600
Morgan just a week earlier during the 1981&nbsp;
bobsled world championships — that prompted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.720
the decision to shorten the Cortina bobsledding&nbsp;
track, where Rigoni was killed, to make it safer.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.160
Filmmaker John Woo was one of the&nbsp;
hottest action directors of the '90s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.920
After a string of groundbreaking&nbsp;
features in his native Hong Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.880
Woo was wooed by Hollywood and came over to&nbsp;
helm such favorites as "Hard Target," "Broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:06.320
Arrow," and "Face/Off." By the late 2000s,&nbsp;
he was back directing smash hits in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.640
and one of his first in his return to his homeland&nbsp;
was "Red Cliff," starring Tony Leung. It was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:14.880
highest-grossing movie of the year in the Middle&nbsp;
Kingdom but came at the cost of a stuntman's life.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.360
A period piece about the battle of China's&nbsp;
Three Kingdoms, the set of the film became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.360
a deadly inferno when a blaze overtook the&nbsp;
production, claiming the life of stuntman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:26.520
Lu Yanqing and injuring six other crew&nbsp;
members. The accident took place during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:31.240
ship battle scene in Xiaotang Shan, just outside&nbsp;
of Beijing. During the filming of the sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:35.520
a smaller vessel engulfed in flames was planned&nbsp;
to collide with a larger ship. But high winds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.360
carried the fire to the second ship and the flames&nbsp;
spread uncontrollably, injuring those onboard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:45.120
killing Yanqing. A statement from the U.S. studio&nbsp;
Lion Rock, which was co-financing the film read,

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.840
"The crew of 'Red Cliff' is deeply&nbsp;
distraught and full of regret."

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:54.360
"Comes a Horseman" was a 1978 Western starring&nbsp;
Jane Fonda, James Caan, and Jason Robards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:59.240
Robards starred as Jacob "J.W." Ewing, a rancher&nbsp;
who wants to protect his sprawling valley from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:03.960
big business tycoons who would try to exploit it&nbsp;
for its natural resources. Working as Robards'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.880
stunt double was stunt actor Jim Sheppard,&nbsp;
who had a wealth of experience on Westerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:12.920
like "The Wild Bunch" and "Hour of the Gun," and&nbsp;
even sci-fi outings like "Planet of the Apes."

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:16.640
In "Comes a Horseman," Sheppard was filming a&nbsp;
scene in which he was supposed to be dragged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:20.720
behind a galloping horse through a fence to&nbsp;
simulate the death and disappearance of Robards'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:25.400
character. However, the horse didn't stick to the&nbsp;
straight path through a gate as planned, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:30.200
unexpected equine maneuvers caused Sheppard to be&nbsp;
thrown off, with his head striking a fence post.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:35.000
While he survived the initial blunt head trauma,&nbsp;
he died not long after being hospitalized. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.440
incident sadly occurred on one of the last days&nbsp;
of filming. Although the scene did cut away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:45.360
the fatal collision, the 41-year-old Sheppard's&nbsp;
last stunt was still included in the picture.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:51.320
"Hi guys! Colonel McCoy. I&nbsp;
was just in the neighborhood."

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.880
Though mostly known for his martial arts&nbsp;
movies, Chuck Norris made plenty of military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.440
action flicks in the '80s, with "Delta&nbsp;
Force" being one of his most memorable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.280
A surprise cult hit that co-starred&nbsp;
Lee Marvin, it got a sequel in 1990,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:06.040
"Delta Force 2: The Colombian Connection."&nbsp;
For the follow-up, it was Norris headlining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:10.680
the action alone as Major Scott McCoy. But&nbsp;
like many other action films, "Delta Force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:15.320
2" also saw a deadly helicopter crash during&nbsp;
filming that took the life of a stunt actor.

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.160
This time, it was in the Philippines,&nbsp;
where the action sequel was shooting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.840
and it sadly wasn't just stuntman Geoff Brewer&nbsp;
who lost his life in the crash. Alongside him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:28.120
were pilots Don Marshall and Jojo Imperiale,&nbsp;
cameraman Gadi Danzig, and key grip Mike Graham.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:32.000
Though only three men died at the scene, at&nbsp;
least two more succumbed to their injuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:36.280
of the nearly a dozen people hospitalized&nbsp;
after the accident. To make matters worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:40.480
the production company — notorious low-budget&nbsp;
studio Cannon Films — had seen the deaths of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.320
at least 10 people while filming numerous&nbsp;
projects over the course of four years.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:49.040
Another film in a long line that saw deadly&nbsp;
helicopter crashes during production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:53.440
"Hired to Kill" is — like "Delta Force&nbsp;
II" — a low-budget action movie from 1990,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:57.960
this one starring Brian Thompson, George Kennedy,&nbsp;
and Oliver Reed. Thompson plays a mercenary who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:02.600
poses as a photographer when he's hired to rescue&nbsp;
a freedom fighter being held behind enemy lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:06.360
During filming the year before the movie hit&nbsp;
theaters, a helicopter being used to shoot a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.960
mid-air scene crashed, injuring the pilot and&nbsp;
several others, and killing a stunt actor.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:14.200
The actor, Clint Carpenter, had been&nbsp;
a regular stuntman on the kid's action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:18.160
series "V.R. Troopers," and was riding with&nbsp;
a pair of fellow stuntmen. While performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:22.320
what was later described as a "routine fly-by,"&nbsp;
moving across the camera's field of vision — set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:26.120
up on a rooftop — the vehicle attempted to&nbsp;
make a tight turn. For unexplained reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:29.520
the helicopter failed to achieve the&nbsp;
turn and crashed into a group of trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:33.840
killing Carpenter, who was pronounced dead&nbsp;
before he even made it to a nearby hospital.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.160
Passengers Michael Kane, pilot Marc Wolff,&nbsp;
and stunt coordinator Mark Cuttin, along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.120
a third unknown stuntman, all sustained injuries.&nbsp;
Though they suffered broken ribs, a leg fracture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.240
and a punctured lung among them, they&nbsp;
were able to escape with their lives.

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:48.600
"Houston, we have a problem."

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:53.920
Before "Apollo 13" in 1995, it was 1983's "The&nbsp;
Right Stuff" that held the title for best film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:58.080
about NASA's space flight program. Oddly&nbsp;
enough, that movie also included "Apollo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.480
13" actor Ed Harris, who plays astronaut John&nbsp;
Glenn. It recounts the years that led up to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:06.360
launching of the Mercury 7, beginning when&nbsp;
Chuck Yeager first broke the sound barrier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:10.560
and includes all the incredibly dangerous&nbsp;
exploits of test pilots like Yeager, Glenn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:14.560
and Alan Shepard. The film features several&nbsp;
incredible high-flying stunts, and one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:19.000
them involves a pilot parachuting to safety&nbsp;
from the confines of an experimental aircraft.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:23.120
Unlike in the movie, though, the pilot never&nbsp;
did make it home safely. Performing the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:26.400
was stunt actor and Houston native&nbsp;
Joseph Svec, both a veteran of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.000
profession and a qualified parachutist.&nbsp;
But during the filming of the ejection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.720
Svec inexplicably failed to pull his ripcord, the&nbsp;
parachute never opened, and he plummeted to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:38.520
death. According to a manager of the California&nbsp;
City Airport where the scene was being filmed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.960
an investigation revealed that nothing was&nbsp;
wrong with Svec's chute, suggesting that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:46.880
was either a simple error by Svec or that he&nbsp;
somehow lost consciousness during the plunge.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:51.440
In the 1930s, Jack Budlong wasn't strictly a&nbsp;
stunt performer; he was also a professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:55.760
horseman and actor. But for the 1941 movie&nbsp;
"They Died With Their Boots On" — a Civil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:59.400
War classic about General Custer that&nbsp;
starred Errol Flynn, Olivia de Havilland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:03.200
and Gene Lockhart — Budlong was brought in for&nbsp;
the film's sprawling war scenes. Reportedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:06.960
Budlong played both Union and Confederate&nbsp;
soldiers at various points in the production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:10.960
and it was during one sequence when he was in the&nbsp;
Southern grays that the horse rider was killed.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:13.640
Budlong, who is said to have been&nbsp;
a close personal friend of Flynn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:17.200
had persuaded the star into getting him a role&nbsp;
as a stunt-performing extra during the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:21.160
war scenes. Not much of a veteran stunt&nbsp;
actor, though, Budlong's inexperience may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:25.160
have been what led him to insist on using&nbsp;
a real saber rather than a prop. So when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:29.840
during filming, his horse began bucking due to&nbsp;
the simulated artillery exploding around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:34.720
Budlong was tossed off the animal and landed&nbsp;
right on his very real, very sharp blade.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:38.440
Budlong was taken to a Los Angeles&nbsp;
hospital, but it was far too late for him:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:42.040
the saber had punctured his abdomen and&nbsp;
exited via his back. He wasn't the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:44.520
casualty of filming, though, as the&nbsp;
production of "They Died With Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:48.160
Boots On" led to countless injuries and&nbsp;
at least two more deaths besides Budlong.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:55.840
"Do you know what is there, waiting beyond&nbsp;
that beach? Immortality! Take it, it's yours!"

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:59.960
Brad Pitt's 2004 ancient war movie&nbsp;
"Troy," directed by Wolfgang Peterson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:04.080
adapted Homer's "Iliad," the epic poem that&nbsp;
depicted the historical Trojan War waged between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.680
the Greeks and the Spartans. Its filming was also&nbsp;
a massive undertaking and a throwback to epics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:13.400
of the '50s and '60, which meant it had several&nbsp;
large-scale action sequences involving hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:18.160
of extras. It also led to the death of one of&nbsp;
the many stunt actors involved in the production.

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:22.040
Stuntman George Camilleri was admitted to a&nbsp;
hospital during filming with "serious trauma"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.880
to his ankle, according to the Times of Malta,&nbsp;
while also suffering chest pains and shortness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:30.120
of breath. He underwent surgery, but two days&nbsp;
later he was dead. Though initially it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:34.520
realized that his death had been related to an&nbsp;
on-set accident, a later inquiry traced the cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:38.880
of Camilleri's death all back to injuries he'd&nbsp;
sustained while filming an action scene on "Troy."

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:43.040
An autopsy revealed that Camilleri's death&nbsp;
was caused by pulmonary thromboembolism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:44.240
with the report stating,

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:48.160
"Pulmonary thromboembolism is not an&nbsp;
uncommon complication following trauma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:51.640
fracture of the lower leg, surgery&nbsp;
to the lower limb and immobility."

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:55.080
According to the investigation, the initial&nbsp;
injury occurred when Calmierri had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:58.840
jumping over the side of a galleon. But&nbsp;
mixed signals between extras led to him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:03.120
making an error and jumping from the wrong&nbsp;
spot, which he hadn't been prepared for.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:06.920
The 1965 film "The Flight of the Phoenix"&nbsp;
starred James Stewart as a pilot whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.920
plane crashes in the Sahara. Desperately&nbsp;
trying to keep himself, his crew and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:14.520
passengers alive in the remote desert, his&nbsp;
only hope is a passenger whose experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:18.240
as an aircraft designer has him convinced they&nbsp;
can build a new plane out of spare parts from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:22.440
the wreck. It's a nail-biting tale of adventure&nbsp;
and survival, but in the making of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:26.360
a famed stunt pilot who was hired to make&nbsp;
risky aerial maneuvers lost his life.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:29.880
An experienced racing pilot who'd&nbsp;
worked in Hollywood since the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:33.360
Paul Mantz was well-known for helping&nbsp;
Amelia Earhart and her navigator Fred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.760
Noonan prepare for their first attempt&nbsp;
to circumnavigate the globe. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:40.280
practically pilot royalty in his day,&nbsp;
but that didn't mean he was invincible.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:44.240
During filming of "The Flight of the Phoenix," he&nbsp;
was flying low in a sequence meant to simulate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:48.240
take-off when something went wrong and the&nbsp;
plane crashed, killing him. Ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.920
it wasn't even Mantz who was supposed to be&nbsp;
behind the controls that day, but his partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:55.920
Frank Tallman. At the last minute, though,&nbsp;
Tallman injured his knee and Mantz filled in.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:59.000
With Mantz's death, "The Flight of the&nbsp;
Phoenix" may have been a cursed production:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:03.280
In 2004, Dennis Quaid starred in a remake&nbsp;
that seriously injured cameraman Ciaran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:07.240
Barry. He received a million-dollar&nbsp;
settlement, though no one was killed.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:11.520
Movies about dangerous professions are&nbsp;
inherently going to involve precarious stunts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:15.040
so when it came time to film a story&nbsp;
about the construction of a skyscraper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:19.160
there was extra danger involved. Experienced&nbsp;
stuntman A.J. Bakunas was doubling for lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:23.760
actor George Kennedy on the Lexington, Kentucky&nbsp;
set of "Steel" when tragedy struck. In the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.160
he attempted a daring drop that&nbsp;
would feature him plunging from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.560
the top of a high-rise to simulate the&nbsp;
falling death of Kennedy's character.

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:33.640
Bakunas was a well-known name in the industry&nbsp;
long before he went to work on the film. By all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:37.600
accounts, he had been preparing for the jump for&nbsp;
weeks and decided to increase the length of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:42.480
fall to 323 feet instead of the ninth-story leap&nbsp;
he'd already completed. He wasn't doing it to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:46.640
the stunt more impressive, but instead simply&nbsp;
hoping to top a stunt performed on another set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:51.120
by rival stuntman Dar Robinson — who was still&nbsp;
nearly a decade away from his own fatal stunt.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:55.640
Bakunas completed the jump as planned, but the&nbsp;
airbag below ruptured, injuring him critically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:59.400
right in front of a massive crowd of spectators&nbsp;
who had assembled to watch the stunt. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:02.600
was still alive and conscious as he was&nbsp;
rushed to the emergency room and treated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:06.240
but he eventually succumbed to&nbsp;
his injuries. Bakunas was only 27.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:10.600
"One thing I'd like to know. Are&nbsp;
world records worth the risk?"

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:15.080
Movies involving high-flying aircraft action can&nbsp;
be fraught with danger and typically require the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.080 --> 00:17:19.160
skills of talented stunt pilots who can pull off&nbsp;
death-defying maneuvers. That was the case with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:24.160
"Von Richthofen and Brown," a 1971 World War I&nbsp;
epic about one of the greatest flying aces that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:28.440
history has ever known. A passion project for&nbsp;
director Roger Corman, who put up much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:32.920
financing himself, the film was shot in Ireland&nbsp;
and told the story of Manfred von Richthofen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:37.160
also known as "The Red Baron," and the pilot Roy&nbsp;
Brown, who took him down. Filming the intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:41.080
dogfights, though, led to a mid-air collision&nbsp;
that killed stuntman Charles Boddington.

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:44.000
Following the incident, the Irish&nbsp;
government decided to shut down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:47.200
production's aerial scenes that Corman&nbsp;
had scheduled for Weston Airfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:51.400
not far from Dublin. According to contemporary&nbsp;
accounts, Boddington's disastrous crash in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:56.240
the sky wasn't even the last accident during&nbsp;
filming. Just a day later, pilot Lynn Garrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.240 --> 00:18:01.680
and actor Don Stroud received injuries after an&nbsp;
unscheduled crash landing in the Liffey River.

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:05.600
It probably isn't much of a surprise to learn&nbsp;
that there have been some mishaps on the sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:10.160
of movies starring Arnold Schwarzenegger. After&nbsp;
all, the overly-muscled body builder-turned-actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:14.360
is known for his explosive action movies,&nbsp;
from science fiction and fantasy to military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:19.320
thrillers. In 1988, filming on his buddy cop&nbsp;
action-comedy "Red Heat" went a little too far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:23.360
even by Arnold's standards, resulting in&nbsp;
the death of stunt actor Bennie Dobbins.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:27.240
The film, which was dedicated to Dobbins,&nbsp;
had Arnold in the role of a Russian military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:31.120
captain named Ivan Danko. He's sent to&nbsp;
Chicago where he teams up with a local cop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:34.520
after they realize they're both looking for&nbsp;
the same criminal. Filled with guns-blazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:39.280
shootouts and bare-knuckle brawls, "Red Heat"&nbsp;
is an underrated Arnie classic. But filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:43.080
one scene that involved the "Terminator" star&nbsp;
duking it out hand-to-hand with a stunt man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:46.520
who wasn't Dobbins, led to a&nbsp;
death in an unexpected way.

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:49.800
During the scene, Dobbins was serving as&nbsp;
the stunt coordinator, helping stage the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:53.680
scene with his two actors when he suddenly&nbsp;
collapsed. Suffering a fatal heart attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:58.560
Dobbins was airlifted to a local hospital&nbsp;
but didn't survive. He was 55 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:03.840
and left behind work in many action classics like&nbsp;
"First Blood," "Commando," and "The Running Man."

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:07.351
"It's time to start running!"

